Die 1 is revealed as 1. The expected sum is 1 + 4.875 + 5.375 + 4.667 ≈ 15.9, and the standard deviation is about 6.5. Noise traders center on 15.9 with sd 5.9.

Averaging over the alpha prior gives E[alpha] ≈ 0.48, and expected profit is linear in alpha, so that average is the right input. Balancing noise-trader spread capture against informed adverse selection puts the optimal quotes about 7.4 to 7.5 on either side of fair value. That gives a bid near 8.5 and an ask near 23.3. The current bid of 14 looks too high, since with this much informed flow it loses money on expectation.

{"bid": 8.5, "ask": 23.3}

Die 1 is revealed as 3. The expected sum is 3 + 4.875 + 5.375 + 4.667 ≈ 17.9, which is the public fair value. The other dice add variance (about 6.5 standard deviation), so informed counterparties can exploit quotes near fair value.

Quoting symmetrically about 17.9, the expected profit per side turns positive only once the quote is about 7 to 8 above or below fair. Using the average alpha (about 0.48) and the noise trader spread of 5.9, the expected profit from each side peaks around ±7.5. That gives roughly 10.4 on the bid and 25.4 on the ask. Earlier wide quotes in this round drew no trades, which gives little reason to tighten.

