Fair value at this stage is 13.0: die 1 averages 9.25 and die 2 averages 3.75, and neither is revealed yet. The sum can only reach 19 at most, so an ask of 19 is almost never hit by an informed buyer. The noise-trader edge at 19 is about 0.2 per quote, versus a net loss from informed trades at 18. A bid of 7 mirrors this, since no informed seller has a value below 7.

The posterior on alpha leans toward the high value after no trades so far, which favours the wide quote.

{"bid": 7, "ask": 19}

Die 2 is still hidden, so the fair value is 9.25 + 3.75 = 13. The settlement distribution runs from 7 to 19, with 7 and 19 each having a 4/32 chance of being the sum at the extremes.

Quoting 7 and 19 means an informed counterparty never trades, since it would need the value below 7 to sell to me or above 19 to buy from me. Only noise traders can hit these quotes, and each one trades at about 6 from fair value. Tightening either side would expose me to informed flow, which costs more than the extra noise volume earns, given that the no-trade history in rounds 1–3 suggests alpha is probably not tiny.

