Fair value is 2 × (152/22) ≈ 13.8, since each card is marginally uniform over the deck and the sum has standard deviation about 7.2. Nothing is revealed yet and no trades have happened this round, so the public fair value is unchanged.

With alpha unknown, I use its mean (about 0.48), since expected profit is linear in alpha. Setting a one-sided offset d from fair value, noise traders pay d per unit with a probability that falls as d grows, while informed traders cost about E[(S − F − d)⁺] per unit. The net is positive and peaks near d ≈ 8, so I quote about 8 either side of fair value.

