Die 3 = 12 is revealed and the contract settles at the largest die, so the settlement value is exactly 12 whatever dice 1 and 2 show. Both informed and noise counterparties are now pricing around a known 12.

I'm short 2, so the sensible quotes are as close to 12 as the tick size allows on each side. A bid of 11.9 buys at a price below 12, so any seller fills at a guaranteed 0.1 per unit. An ask of 12.1 sells above 12, so any buyer fills at a guaranteed 0.1 per unit. Informed traders won't trade against either quote, since they know the value is 12.

{"bid": 11.9, "ask": 12.1}

Settlement is 12 with certainty: die 1 and die 2 are at most 10, so the largest die is die 3 = 12. Informed counterparties know the value is 12, so they never trade with a bid below 12 or an ask above 12. Only noise traders can trade with quotes on those sides, and they trade with probability that falls as the quote moves away from 12.

The expected profit per quote on the ask side is (A − 12) × (1 − α) × P(noise valuation > A), and the bid side is symmetric. Maximizing this with noise sd 2.1 gives an offset of about 1.58 from 12. Rounding to the tick grid gives an ask of 13.6 (rounded up) and a bid of 10.4 (rounded down), each 1.6 from 12. The result doesn't depend on α, since informed traders never trade at these prices.

{"bid": 10.4, "ask": 13.6}

Die 3 is 12 and no other die can exceed 12, so the settlement value is 12 with certainty. Public fair value is 12, and informed counterparties can only trade against a quote that is on the wrong side of 12. With bid below 12 and ask above 12, no informed trade is profitable for them.

The remaining question is how far from 12 to quote to maximise expected profit against noise traders. On the bid side, the profit is x·Φ(-x/2.1), where x = 12 - bid. Maximising this gives x ≈ 1.6, so the bid is about 10.4. By symmetry the ask is about 13.6. This matches the quote I posted at quote 3 of this round.

