The coin posterior after flips 1-3 (one head, two tails) is about 0.51 for p=0.2 and 0.49 for p=0.5. The settlement is 10 × (1 + heads in flips 4 and 5), so it can only be 10, 20 or 30. Those probabilities are roughly 0.45, 0.41 and 0.14.

Because the value is always 10, 20 or 30, an informed counterparty only trades against a quote that is inside one of those levels. A bid of 10 is never sold into, since the value is never below 10. An ask of 30 is never bought from, since the value is never above 30. So this quote earns the edge only on noise trades, and an informed counterparty cannot trade against it.

